          title: "“He loved them to the end”",
          body: "The verse is the doorway into the second half of John’s Gospel — the passing from the *Book of Signs* to the *Book of Glory* — and it holds the whole passion in a single sentence about love. Jesus **knows** everything: that his hour has come, that he is going to the Father, that betrayal sits at his own table. And knowing all of it, he loves. The little phrase *eis telos* rings two bells at once: **“to the end”** in time — he loved them right through to the last, into death itself; and **“to the uttermost”** in degree — completely, perfectly, holding nothing back. The same word *telestai,* “it is finished,” will fall from the cross (19:30); here at the supper the love that will be *finished* there is already fully in motion. And note the sheer particularity of it: not humanity in the abstract but **“his own who were in the world”** — this frightened, dull, quarrelsome band, Judas among them as the basin is filled. He loved *these*, to the end. What follows — the towel, the bread, the long farewell — is simply that love unfolding into gesture and word. Everything in the upper room is a commentary on this line.",
