      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a13.27.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g13.27.10', 'g13.27.18', 'g13.30.9'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“And it was night”",
          body: "The morsel is a gesture of honor — the host dipping bread and handing it to a favored guest — and Jesus offers it to his betrayer: one last, wordless appeal of love. Judas takes the bread of friendship and, in the same moment, opens himself wholly to the darkness: **“Satan entered into him.”** John does not excuse Judas by blaming the devil, nor blame the devil to excuse Judas; the two consent together. Then Jesus, unbroken and in command, releases him: **“What you are going to do, do quickly.”** He is not Judas’ victim but the Shepherd who lays down his life *of his own accord* (10:18) — even his betrayal moves on his word, at his hour. And Judas goes out into the dark. John’s last four words are among the most freighted in all his Gospel: **“And it was night.”** Literally true — but John never wastes a detail of light and darkness. Judas leaves the room where the Light of the world sits, steps past the threshold of grace, and enters the night he has chosen. It is the deepest midnight a soul can walk into: to turn one’s back on Love itself, with the taste of his bread still in one’s mouth.",
        },
      ],
